Z||000005||Patrick||09-05-2000||09:15 PM||||<BR>Johnny, the problem with you starting several threads about the same topic is that I think you're forgetting what you've already been told. In a previous thread I stated:  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I'm not sure if you're planing to try and replace parts of the line (rigid steel vs flexible hose) individually or what. With all the trouble you've been having, I think you better buy the one piece unit that has all the sections already connected together.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR><B>Bite the bullet and buy the proper hydraulic line</B>. Otherwise, you're going to be posting here indefinitely and never getting a chance to drive that Fiero..||||reg||1
Z||000006||wilburt||09-05-2000||09:26 PM||||Here's what I would do...<P>Go to the bone yard, find the best flex hose you can find, (on a fiero!). Below the bracket, where it turns into metal line, reach as far down the metel line as you can, and cut the metal line. Bracket, flex hose, metal line should cost about 5 bucks. Take this assembly to your auto parts store, and purchas the correct size 'UNION'. This pipe fitting will join the metal line you have to the metal line on your car. Hopefull the parts house can help you with making sure the Union is of the correct rating for PSI.<BR>Hope this helps you.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000007||Patrick||09-05-2000||09:34 PM||||<BR>For gawd's sake Wilbert, don't encourage Johnny to piecemeal this hydraulic line together. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> He'll be fixing leaks and we'll be hearing about it for the rest of the year. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
